 breaching the door and  entering the residence  as a matter of practice in in my  practice if i come into a case early on  early enough where i can send an  investigator out to see if there's  any evidence to show the police entry  or show who's out there or any type of  you know looking for things that may  help the case and kind of shed light to  see if the police did their job properly  or not  and it showed a completely different um  scenario or set of facts than what's in  the police report  uh so then we filed a motion to suppress  and during this  the suppression hearing the police  officer testified untruthfully by  basically saying  he walks up to the door with his um  colleagues behind him  uh and then he steps up on on the porch  uh knocks loudly with his big hands  waits 10 to 20 seconds or 10 to 15  seconds  you know depending on which version he  went with first or second  and then he knocks again and you know  during the knocks they announced that  police were there for the search warrant  and he said he did that loudly  uh and um once he testified to those  facts i got up to cross-examine him  knowing what actually did happen because  i  you know i have the the surveillance uh  video  and i started to question him about that  and  immediately i think he probably realized  that i have something that he's not  aware of  and immediately began to back up on his  initial testimony  so procedurally here this is what's  amazing to me the prosecutor is the one  who  originally elicited the testimony from  the police that this is exactly how this  went down  not knowing that you in your proverbial  back pocket have this video  that you're going to bring out that's  correct  the judge ruled that it was the  prosecutor's obligation to put on a  witness  to show that the execution of the law  was proper i wanted him to tell me his  version  open-ended i didn't want to i didn't  want to ask leading questions initially  and that's what the prosecutor did for  me 